                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Your Appeal Failed Because of Method, Not Sincerity</h2>
<p>Among the Google Ads account suspension cases we've handled, over 60% failed on first appeal. And of those failures, at least 70% weren't due to serious policy violations—they failed because the appeal materials missed the mark.</p>
<p>This isn't users' fault. Google's appeal portal provides only a text box with zero guidance on what to submit. Most appeal letters spend excessive space on apologies and promises, which convey no actionable information to reviewers.</p>
<p><strong>A effective appeal must contain three core modules</strong>: root cause analysis, corrective action checklist, and future prevention mechanism. Let's break down each.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 1: Root Cause Analysis — Specifics, Not Surface Descriptions</h2>
<p>Many users describe violations vaguely: "Our ad content may not have complied with Google's policies." This says nothing.</p>
<p>Reviewers need to see: which specific ad, which creative, which landing page violated which policy—and why you only discovered the issue after the fact.</p>
<p><strong>Effective example</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Upon internal review, our travel app ad (Campaign ID: xxx) used the phrase 'guaranteed lowest price,' which violates Google's policy on prohibited 'price guarantee claims.' This issue arose because the creative team did not include price-related language in their compliance checklist, resulting in direct launch without legal approval."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This format accomplishes three things: identifies the specific violation, references the relevant policy, and explains the root cause.</p>
<p>If your account was suspended due to <strong>payment or billing issues</strong> (such as multiple accounts sharing the same payment method), your root cause analysis must list all associated account IDs and explain their relationships. Many users try to hide these connections—don't. Google's review systems can detect what you're trying to hide. Proactive disclosure is always better than being caught.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 2: Corrective Action Checklist — Timestamps and Evidence</h2>
<p>Saying "we will correct this" is insufficient. Reviewers need to see what you've already done.</p>
<p><strong>Your corrective actions must include two elements</strong>: specific operational steps and corresponding completion dates or supporting materials.</p>
<p>A utility app team provided the following checklist in their appeal and received a positive response within 48 hours:</p>
<ul>
<li>All historical ad creatives containing superlatives ('best,' 'first,' etc.) have been taken offline (screenshots archived)</li>
<li>Updated creative review process with new compliance checkpoint; responsible party: Content Director</li>
<li>Scheduled third-party policy compliance training, expected completion next week</li>
</ul>
<p>Note: each item is a verifiable action, not vague statements like "strengthen learning" or "raise awareness."</p>
<p>For <strong>sensitive industries</strong> (finance, healthcare, gaming), your corrective actions should include industry-specific certifications or compliance documentation. Reviewers handle numerous cases in these verticals and know exactly what credentials are required.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Module 3: Future Prevention Mechanism — Give Reviewers a Reason to Trust You Again</h2>
<p>This is the most overlooked but highest-weighted module.</p>
<p>Google's review logic isn't about punishment—it's about assessing your probability of future violations. If you can demonstrate a systematic prevention mechanism, reviewers are more inclined to give you another chance.</p>
<p><strong>Prevention mechanisms hinge on documented SOPs</strong>. Consider this approach:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"We have established a 'Google Ads Creative Compliance Review SOP,' requiring all ad creatives to go through: ① Policy clause verification → ② Team lead review → ③ Archive documentation before launch. This SOP has been trained across the team and incorporated into performance reviews."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If your team is small and can't implement a full process, demonstrate active policy learning—such as subscribing to Google Ads policy updates or regularly attending Google's official advertising policy workshops.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Final Reminder: One Appeal Cycle Is All You Need</h2>
<p>We've seen users submit the same appeal four or five times for one account, simply rewriting apologies each time. The result? The account escalated from "temporary restriction" to "permanent suspension."</p>
<p>The first appeal is the most critical and receives the most careful review. If the first appeal is rejected, the second must include new evidence or circumstances not previously provided—not the same message in different wording.</p>
<p>When preparing appeal materials, answer three questions first: Why was my account suspended? What have I done to correct it? How will I prevent this from happening again? If you can clearly answer these three questions, your appeal materials are 80% complete.</p>
<p>If you're struggling with a Google Ads account appeal, contact 以渔数媒's customer service team. We've handled account reinstatement cases across multiple industries and can provide guidance on appeal preparation and submission.</p>
